text:
maria: hitting a weird JVM client bug with streaming SSE -> Java NIO InputStream -> Jackson parsing. chunks sometimes split JSON objects and decoder throws on malformed UTF-8. anyone seen this pattern?

devin: yup. we had two issues: 1) bytes split in the middle of UTF-8 codepoint, 2) parser seeing partial JSON. what client stack are you using?

maria: using java.net.http WebSocket -> BodyHandler.ofLines + custom ByteBuffer collector. essentially reading ByteBuffers and handing to a single-threaded parser.

jen: suggestion: use a CharsetDecoder with decode(ByteBuffer, CharBuffer, false) to handle split codepoints. decode(in, out, false) and then flush at end. avoids illegal char exceptions when a multibyte rune is split across chunks. :sparkles:

akash: +1 for CharsetDecoder. also consider Jackson non-blocking parser (JsonFactory.createNonBlockingByteArrayParser). it has an InputFeeder where you feed partial byte arrays and it produces tokens only when complete. works well for partial JSON.

maria: interesting. can you paste a tiny example of the non-blocking feeder usage?

akash: sure, minimal sketch:

```
JsonFactory jf = new JsonFactory();
NonBlockingByteArrayParser p = (NonBlockingByteArrayParser) jf.createNonBlockingByteArrayParser();
byte[] chunk = ...; // chunk from socket
p.getNonBlockingInputFeeder().feedInput(chunk, 0, chunk.length);
JsonToken t;
while ((t = p.nextToken()) != null) {
  // handle token
}
if (p.getNonBlockingInputFeeder().needMoreInput()) {
  // wait for next chunk
}
```

jen: note that you still need to handle UTF-8 boundaries if you feed bytes directly. Jackson expects full bytes (it will surface errors if input is truncated mid-codepoint). If the server stream may break multibyte sequences, use a decoder layer first or buffer trailing bytes until you have a complete sequence.

devin: another pragmatic approach: if you control the server, switch to NDJSON or add a lightweight delimiter (\n\n) between objects. that makes client logic much simpler — treat each line as an independent JSON doc.

maria: server is third-party, can't change. currently we accumulated bytes into a bounded queue and attempted to find balanced braces via a simple brace counter. worked in a pinch but brittle for strings containing braces or escaped sequences.

akash: brace counting is fragile (strings, escapes, nested arrays). if you must, combine brace counting with a simple state machine that tracks when you're inside quotes and handles backslashes. still hacky. prefer using a real parser.

jen: about backpressure: java.net.http's BodyHandler doesn't give good manual backpressure controls. consider switching to a reactive stack (HttpClient + Reactor Netty / AsyncHttpClient) where you can apply a small buffer size and use request(n) to control demand, or implement a bounded queue and pause reading from the socket until the queue drains.

devin: if switching stacks is heavy, implement read-side backpressure by having the read thread feed a BlockingQueue with capacity N and have parser thread poll. when queue.put() blocks the socket reader will naturally stall. works if your HTTP client uses a thread that blocks on your consumer; less reliable if client reads into memory regardless.

maria: good point. our client uses a callback that always hands bytes to us; we then enqueue — but callback runs on Netty IO so we must avoid blocking there. we had a bug where we blocked Netty event loop.

jen: never block Netty. instead use an SpscRingBuffer or a bounded non-blocking queue and apply backpressure by dropping or signalling upstream (if possible). or offload to a dedicated blocking thread via ExecutorService and let that thread block on a bounded queue.put().

akash: also consider a small RateLimiter or a semaphore controlling number of in-flight bytes being processed. metric: track queuedBytes and reject new read callbacks until queuedBytes < threshold. implement the reject by storing the partial ByteBuffer and returning control (if client supports pull). if client is push-only, offload to blocking thread.

maria: ok plan: switch to NonBlockingByteArrayParser + CharsetDecoder as a buffer for trailing bytes + dedicated parser thread that drains a BlockingQueue with capacity 8 and blocks the reader thread by handing queue.put() to a separate single-threaded executor. that avoids blocking Netty event loop. also add a small integration test to simulate split multibyte sequences.

devin: tests: write a tiny SSE test server that emits a multibyte UTF-8 char split across two writes and a JSON object split across two writes. assert parser produces the correct object and no UTF exceptions. CI should run it.

ci-bot: created task: devex/streaming-java-integration-test #2383 (assignee: maria). test runs in CI matrix linux/java17. :white_check_mark:

maria: thanks folks. I'll prototype tonight and post a short PR. if anyone wants to pair on the racey parts ping me. :coffee:
